 actual full-fledged  beta release but a lot of the work is  done and so it's actually this work this  sort of thing works where you can  install the entire suite of software  tools with a one-liner dependencies are  handled automatically and so it seems to  be a pretty reasonable sort of approach  when the change gears one last time and  talk about how we might use these models  and falsify them in the wet lab so I've  told you about a lot of computational  models but models are really useless if  you have no way to validate them to  falsify them or refine them and and this  sort of validation occurs by making real  measurements in the wet lab but that's  really expensive so chemistry and bio  chemistry biology and physics they're  really labor-intensive things to do and  we're talking about models with  thousands of parameters which means you  probably need to be making thousands of  measurements so we have a really massive  challenge with reproducibility and  scalability so work in the Kidero lab  and this is work from yon Hendrik Prince  bas rustenburg Sonia Hansen they've been  developing software libraries to make  experiments as easy as programming in  Python so the idea is that you can  import some modules you can instantiate  some classes and kind of string them  together to actually perform experiments  in the wet lab and these experiments are  going to be performed on a robot that  looks something like this  but this is actually building the robot  that we have at memorial sloan-kettering  what no no they're just fast forwarded I  cannot  alright so yeah that's basically our the  robot that we're using and see sorry  about the volume but it was my poor  little macbook air speakers but just to  leave you I just want to give you our  vision for what biophysical modeling and  experiment should be and that we really  want it to be reproducible automated  testable and really just useful for  making predictions and testing them and  with that I just want to tha